{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The relation kind 'shares mechanism' is misapplied. SRC_001 describes a unidirectional causal relationship — the harness's criticism loop produces isolation in the artifact — yet labels it 'shares mechanism,' which implies the isolated problem 0e26d6be54fd is a co-participant in the criticism mechanism. The isolated problem is a passive outcome of the harness design, not an entity that possesses or exercises the conjecture-criticism loop. A 'depends on' or 'caused by' relation would accurately capture the described dynamic; 'shares mechanism' inflates a one-directional production relationship into a bidirectional shared property. The counterexample: a program that crashes due to a bug in its runtime does not 'share mechanism' with the runtime — it is produced by it. The refuted-if clause does not rescue this mislabeling because even if the harness amendment lacks an integration mandate, the relation kind remains mischaracterized.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A target artifact A becomes isolated because framework F's adjudication loop rejects all candidates connecting A to its neighbourhood. Saying A 'shares mechanism' with F is incorrect: A does not exercise the adjudication loop, F does. The correct relation is 'A depends on F's mechanism' or 'F's mechanism causes A's isolation.'"},{"scenario":"A ball falls because gravity acts on it. The ball does not 'share mechanism' with gravity; it is subject to gravity's mechanism."}],"target":"8a45f50ab8164d3bafb06e312df7100877c707dc0ee94ca564aaa5a3f31077c8"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"b72e4f0f5f8cc90778dd2260fe9c0b5dae4b2aba0a9f94c936b7431bcafb8a72"}]}